const bodyTop = `
<section class="seo-hero">
 <div class="seo-hero-bg"></div>
 <div class="seo-hero-ov"></div>
 <div class="seo-hero-cnt">
  <p class="eyebrow">Dinosaur National Monument — Utah Side</p>
  <h1>Visiting Dinosaur National Monument<br><em>from Jensen &amp; Vernal</em></h1>
  <p>Jensen is the gateway to the Utah side of the monument — and it has no hotels. This is a practical guide to the fees, the shuttle, the Cub Creek drive, and where to base yourself.</p>
  <div class="seo-hero-btns">
   <a href="tel:${BUSINESS.phoneTel}" class="btn-p">📞 ${BUSINESS.directBooking.ctaLabel} ${BUSINESS.directBooking.discountPercent}%</a>
   <a href="tel:${BUSINESS.phoneTel}" class="btn-s">📞 ${BUSINESS.phoneDisplay}</a>
  </div>
 </div>
</section>

<div class="content-wrap">

<div class="intro-pull"><strong>There are no hotels in Jensen, Utah.</strong> That is the single most useful fact for anyone planning this trip, and it is why almost everyone visiting the Utah side of Dinosaur National Monument sleeps in Vernal, about 13 miles west on US-40. Jensen is the turning — the Quarry Visitor Center is 7 miles north of it on Utah State Highway 149.<sup>1</sup> This page covers what you actually need to plan: fees, how the Exhibit Hall is reached, what else is on the Utah side, and how many days to allow.</div>

<div class="content-section">
 <h2>1. What Dinosaur National Monument Is — and Which Side You Want</h2>
<p>Dinosaur National Monument straddles the Utah–Colorado border, and it has two visitor centers in two different states. This trips up more first-time visitors than anything else about the park.<sup>1</sup></p>
<ul class="feat-list">
 <li><strong>Quarry Visitor Center — Utah side</strong>, reached via Utah State Highway 149 from Jensen. This is the side with the dinosaur bones.<sup>1</sup></li>
 <li><strong>Canyon Visitor Center — Colorado side</strong>, near Dinosaur, Colorado. Canyon and river scenery, no quarry.<sup>1</sup></li>
</ul>
<p>The fossils — the reason most people come — are on the <strong>Utah</strong> side, at the Quarry Exhibit Hall, where roughly 1,500 dinosaur bones remain embedded in the cliff face rather than removed to a museum. If you have driven to Dinosaur, Colorado expecting the wall of bones, you are about two hours from it.</p>
<p><strong>Disambiguation.</strong> This page is about Dinosaur National Monument in Uintah County, Utah and Moffat County, Colorado — the one reached from Jensen, Utah. It is not Dinosaur Valley State Park in Texas, Dinosaur Provincial Park in Alberta, or Dinosaur Ridge in Colorado, all of which are separate places with similar names.</p>
</div>

<div class="content-section">
 <h2>2. How Far It Is From Vernal</h2>
<p>Jensen sits on US-40 roughly 13 miles east of Vernal. The Quarry Visitor Center is a further 7 miles north of Jensen on Utah State Highway 149,<sup>1</sup> making the total from ${BUSINESS.address.full} approximately 20 miles — call it half an hour with the turn. Distances here are approximate; the verified leg is the 7 miles from Jensen to the visitor center.</p>
<p>Because Jensen has no lodging, groceries or full services, treat it as a waypoint rather than a destination: fuel and provision in Vernal, then drive out.</p>
</div>

<div class="content-section">
 <h2>3. Fees and How You Reach the Exhibit Hall</h2>
<p>Entrance is by pass, and the National Park Service publishes the amounts:<sup>2</sup></p>
<ul class="feat-list">
 <li><strong>$25.00</strong> — one private vehicle (15 passengers or fewer) and all occupants, valid 7 consecutive days<sup>2</sup></li>
 <li><strong>$20.00</strong> — motorcycle, valid 7 days, admits up to 2 motorcycles with up to 4 total passengers<sup>2</sup></li>
 <li><strong>$15.00</strong> — per person without a vehicle (cyclists, walkers). Youth 15 and under free<sup>2</sup></li>
 <li><strong>$45.00</strong> — annual Dinosaur pass, valid one year through the month of purchase<sup>2</sup></li>
 <li><strong>$80.00</strong> — America the Beautiful annual pass, covering federal sites nationwide<sup>2</sup></li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Getting to the Exhibit Hall.</strong> Parking at the Exhibit Hall itself is limited, so access depends on season: in summer a free shuttle carries visitors from the Quarry Visitor Center up to the hall, while outside peak season you can generally drive.<sup>1</sup> Shuttle operation and hours change year to year — confirm on the Park Service site before you set out rather than relying on this page.</p>
</div>

<div class="content-section">
 <h2>4. Things To Do on the Utah Side</h2>
<ul class="feat-list">
 <li><strong>Quarry Exhibit Hall</strong> — roughly 1,500 dinosaur bones still in the rock face, the reason the monument exists</li>
 <li><strong>Tour of the Tilted Rocks</strong> — a 10-mile scenic drive along Cub Creek Road, beginning at the Quarry Visitor Center and ending at Josie's cabin. Guidebooks are sold at either visitor center or at a self-service post near the entrance station<sup>3</sup></li>
 <li><strong>Cub Creek petroglyphs</strong> — Fremont rock art distinguished by unusually large lizard figures, reached by a short but steep 0.2-mile trail<sup>3</sup></li>
 <li><strong>Josie Bassett Morris homestead</strong> — Josie settled at Cub Creek in 1914 and lived there independently for about 50 years; the cabin, chicken coop, irrigation remnants and orchard trees survive<sup>3</sup></li>
 <li><strong>Split Mountain and the Green River</strong> — river access, rafting put-ins and the eroded Split Mountain formation</li>
</ul>
</div>

<div class="content-section">
 <h2>5. Seasonal Planning</h2>
<ul class="feat-list">
 <li><strong>Summer</strong> — peak season. Expect the shuttle to the Exhibit Hall rather than driving,<sup>1</sup> and expect company on the Cub Creek drive. Campground reservations are advised.<sup>4</sup></li>
 <li><strong>Spring and autumn</strong> — quieter, and you can often drive to the hall. Campground water and flush toilets are seasonal at the Utah-side campgrounds.<sup>4</sup></li>
 <li><strong>Winter</strong> — the quietest time at the quarry. Services reduce and road conditions vary; check current conditions with the Park Service before travelling.</li>
</ul>
<div class="pro-tip"><p><strong>Two things change without notice:</strong> shuttle operation and road status. Both are published by the National Park Service at nps.gov/dino, and both are worth a look the morning you go. The monument's information line is (435) 781-7700.<sup>1</sup></p></div>
</div>

<div class="content-section">
 <h2>6. Permits, Camping and Regulations</h2>
<p>We link these rather than restate them, because fees, hours and river-permit rules change:</p>
<ul class="feat-list">
 <li><strong>Park hours, fees, shuttle operation and current conditions</strong> — <a href="https://www.nps.gov/dino/index.htm" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:700;">Dinosaur National Monument, National Park Service</a></li>
 <li><strong>Camping</strong> — <strong>Green River Campground</strong> has 83 sites at about 4,750 ft, listed at $24 per night, with reservations advised in peak season; <strong>Split Mountain Group Campground</strong> is open year round for groups. Book via <a href="https://www.recreation.gov" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:700;">Recreation.gov</a><sup>4</sup></li>
 <li><strong>Fishing licences</strong> on the Green River — <a href="https://wildlife.utah.gov/guidebooks" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:700;">Utah Division of Wildlife Resources</a></li>
</ul>
</div>

<div class="content-section">
 <h2>7. Planning Your Visit</h2>
<h3>How many days should you allow?</h3>
<p>The Exhibit Hall plus the 10-mile Cub Creek drive with stops<sup>3</sup> is a comfortable full day. Add a second day if you want the Colorado side, a river trip, or unhurried hiking. One night in Vernal covers a single quarry day; two nights is the realistic minimum if you want more than the fossils.</p>
<h3>Can you combine it with Ashley National Forest?</h3>
<p>Yes, and it is the natural pairing from a Vernal base. The monument is east through Jensen; <a href="/hotel-near-ashley-national-forest" style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:700;">Ashley National Forest</a> is north on US-191. Opposite directions, so plan them as separate days rather than one loop.</p>
<h3>What should you plan for?</h3>
<ul class="feat-list">
 <li><strong>Buy fuel and food in Vernal</strong> — Jensen has no full services and the monument has none</li>
 <li><strong>Carry water</strong> — this is high desert; the quarry area is exposed</li>
 <li><strong>Mobile coverage</strong> — treat it as unreliable once you leave US-40. Cell service at the Utah-side campgrounds is described as seasonal.<sup>4</sup> Download directions before leaving town</li>
 <li><strong>Arrive with a pass or means to buy one</strong> — see the fees above<sup>2</sup></li>
</ul>
</div>

<div class="content-section">
 <h2>8. Staying in Vernal</h2>
